    <div class="content-body" style="margin-top: 30px;">
        <h1 style="margin-bottom: 10px;">District 21 Leadership Directory</h1>
        <p class="lead">Use this directory to contact the Committee on Special Education (CSE) or CPSE offices in NYC District District 21. Understanding the roles of the CSE and CPSE is crucial for effectively navigating the special education system. The CPSE (Committee on Preschool Special Education) handles cases for children aged 3-5, while the CSE (Committee on Special Education) handles cases for school-aged children (5-21). Familiarize yourself with the specific committee relevant to your child's age.</p>
        
        <div class="contact-grid">
            
            <div class="contact-box">
                <h3>CSE 7 (Serving District 21)</h3>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📍 Walk-in Center:</strong><br>415 89th Street, Brooklyn, NY 11209</div>
                <div class="contact-item" style="margin-bottom: 10px;"><strong>📞 Phone:</strong><br><a href="tel:718-759-4900">718-759-4900</a></div>
                <div class="contact-item"><strong>📧 Official Email:</strong><br><a href="mailto:N/A">Contact via NYC Schools Account</a></div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="alert-box" style="background: #fff3cd; padding: 20px; margin-top: 30px; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 5px solid #ffc107;">
            <strong>⚠️ Escalation Tip:</strong> If you do not receive a response within 48 hours, document your attempt and escalate to the NYC DOE Central Region Office. Document date, time, person spoken to (if any), and the content of the conversation. Save all email correspondence.
        </div>

        
<section class="protocol-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-clipboard-check"></i> Communication Protocol
    </h2>
    
    <div class="protocol-grid" style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(300px, 1fr)); gap: 30px;">
        
        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">The "Written Notice" Rule</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                <strong>Never rely on a phone call alone.</strong> In New York Special Education law, timelines (like the 60-day evaluation window as per NYS Part 200.4(a)) only officially start when the district receives a request <em>in writing</em>.
            </p>
            <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li>Always follow up a phone call with an email summary.</li>
                <li>Use the subject line: <em>"Letter of Referral - [Student Name]"</em></li>
                <li>Keep a "Communication Log" of dates and names. This log should include the method of communication (e.g., email, phone), the person you communicated with, a brief summary of the conversation, and any agreed-upon actions.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="protocol-card">
            <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">When to Escalate?</h3>
            <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
                If you contact the leadership listed above and do not receive a response, follow this escalation ladder:
            </p>
            <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
                <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> Initial Email/Phone Call.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> Follow-up Email (CC the Principal and your Parent Member).</li>
                <li><strong>Day 5:</strong> Escalation Email to the Superintendent or District Superintendent. Also, consider contacting the NYC DOE's Office of Student Enrollment.</li>
                <li><strong>Day 10:</strong> File a "State Complaint" with NYSED if services are denied or timelines are violated (see NYS Part 200.5(l)). You can also consider a due process complaint.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>

    </div>
</section>

<section class="evaluation-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-file-alt"></i> Understanding the Initial Evaluation Process
    </h2>

    <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
        The initial evaluation is the cornerstone of special education eligibility. It's crucial to understand your rights and responsibilities during this process, as outlined by the Individuals with Disabilities Education Act (IDEA) and New York State regulations.
    </p>

    <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Step-by-Step Guide to Requesting an Initial Evaluation:</h3>
    <ol style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li><strong>Step 1: Written Request:</strong> Submit a formal, written request for an initial evaluation to the CSE. Clearly state your concerns about your child's academic, social, emotional, or physical development. Be specific about the areas of concern.</li>
        <li><strong>Step 2: District Response:</strong> The district must respond to your request within a specific timeframe (typically 10 school days). They will either provide you with consent forms for evaluation or explain why they are denying the request (which they must do in writing, providing reasons).</li>
        <li><strong>Step 3: Informed Consent:</strong> Carefully review all consent forms before signing. You have the right to understand the purpose of each evaluation, who will be conducting it, and how the results will be used.  If you don't understand something, ask for clarification.</li>
        <li><strong>Step 4: Evaluation Completion:</strong> Once you provide consent, the district has 60 school days to complete the evaluations and hold a CSE meeting (see NYS Part 200.4(a)). This timeline is critical; track it carefully.</li>
        <li><strong>Step 5: CSE Meeting:</strong> Attend the CSE meeting to discuss the evaluation results and determine if your child is eligible for special education services.  Bring any private evaluations, observations, or other relevant information to the meeting.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #002868;">Parent Advocacy Tip: Independent Educational Evaluations (IEEs)</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; color: #444;">
        If you disagree with the district's evaluation, you have the right to request an Independent Educational Evaluation (IEE) at public expense (see IDEA regulations 34 CFR § 300.502). The district must either grant your request or initiate a due process hearing to demonstrate that their evaluation was appropriate.  If granted, the IEE results must be considered by the CSE.
    </p>

    <h3 style="font-size: 1.2rem; color: #c8102e;">Common District Pitfalls to Watch Out For:</h3>
    <ul style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li><strong>Delaying Evaluations:</strong> Districts sometimes try to delay the evaluation process. Be persistent and document all communication.</li>
        <li><strong>Inadequate Evaluations:</strong> Ensure the evaluations are comprehensive and address all areas of concern.  If you believe an evaluation is missing a critical component (e.g., a functional behavioral assessment), request it in writing.</li>
        <li><strong>Predetermined Outcomes:</strong>  Be wary if it seems the CSE has already made a decision before the evaluations are even completed.  The evaluation results should drive the IEP development, not the other way around.</li>
        <li><strong>Failure to Provide Written Notice:</strong> Any decisions made by the CSE MUST be followed by Prior Written Notice (PWN) explaining the decision, the reasons for it, other options considered and rejected, and all evaluation data used to come to the decision.</li>
    </ul>
</section>

<section class="faq-section" style="margin-top: 50px; border-top: 1px solid #eee; padding-top: 40px;">
    <h2 style="color: #002868; font-size: 1.8rem; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <i class="fas fa-question-circle"></i> Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs)
    </h2>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #002868;">Q: What if my child is already receiving academic intervention services (AIS)? Does that mean they don't need a special education evaluation?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
            A: Not necessarily. AIS is a general education intervention. If your child continues to struggle despite receiving AIS, it may be indicative of a disability that requires special education services. Requesting an evaluation is essential to determine if your child needs an IEP. Per NYS Part 200 regulations, simply providing general education interventions does not negate the school's obligation to evaluate a student suspected of having a disability.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #002868;">Q: Can I record my child's CSE meeting?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
            A: In New York State, you generally need the consent of all parties to record a meeting. It's best practice to request permission in writing from the CSE chairperson in advance. If consent is denied, you can still take detailed notes during the meeting. Bring a trusted friend or advocate to assist with note-taking. If the school district is recording, they must inform you.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #002868;">Q: What is the role of the Parent Member on the CSE?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
            A: The Parent Member is a crucial member of the CSE. They are a parent of a child with a disability who is not attending or working for the school district. Their role is to provide a parent's perspective, advocate for the child's needs, and ensure that the IEP is appropriate. They can also provide valuable insights and support to other parents. You can often find the parent member contact information listed with the other CSE staff.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #002868;">Q: What if the CSE recommends a program that I don't think is appropriate for my child?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
            A: You have the right to disagree with the CSE's recommendations. Express your concerns clearly and provide specific reasons why you believe the proposed program is not appropriate. You can request that the CSE consider alternative placements or services. If you are still not satisfied, you have the right to pursue dispute resolution options, such as mediation or a due process hearing. Remember to request Prior Written Notice of the program recommendation.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item" style="margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h3 style="font-size: 1.1rem; color: #002868;">Q: My child has an IEP, but we are moving to District 21 from another district. What happens to the IEP?</h3>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9rem; color: #555;">
            A: When your child moves to District 21, the district must provide comparable services to those outlined in your child's existing IEP until they either adopt the existing IEP or develop a new one. It is crucial to provide the CSE with a copy of your child's IEP and all relevant documentation as soon as possible. Schedule a CSE meeting to discuss your child's needs and ensure a smooth transition. According to IDEA, the new school district must make reasonable efforts to implement the existing IEP.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>
    </div>
</main>
